[AIMessage] **Ring of Nestor**

The Ring of Nestor is the conventional name used in archaeological scholarship 
for a ring from the Bronze Age Aegean that has attracted attention for its 
iconography and for the interpretive questions surrounding its imagery.[1] It 
is discussed in relation to Aegean seal stones, Pylos, and the wider visual 
culture of the Late Bronze Age eastern Mediterranean.[1][4]

== Archaeological context ==
Pylos, on the hill of Ano Englianos in Messenia, was a major Mycenaean palatial
center later associated with the Palace of Nestor.[3] Excavations in 2015 near 
Tholos Tomb IV uncovered the grave of the so-called Griffin Warrior, a richly 
furnished burial of an adult male that contained many objects of gold, silver, 
bronze, ivory, and semiprecious stone, including four gold signet rings 
decorated with Minoan ritual scenes.[2]

== Iconography and interpretation ==
A 2013 study by Theodore Eliopoulos focused on the ring’s iconography and 
placed it within broader Aegean discussions of seal imagery.[1] Later 
scholarship on combat, myth, and seals compared the ring with other Aegean 
objects and treated it as part of a wider visual and cultural tradition linking
Crete and the mainland.[4] The ring has also been discussed in scholarship on 
iconographic comparison and authenticity debates.[1]

== References ==
[1] 
https://www.academia.edu/83658257/SOME_OBSERVATIONS_ON_THE_ICONOGRAPHY_OF_THE_R
ING_OF_NESTOR_

[2] https://www.academia.edu/116392582/The_Lord_of_the_Gold_Rings

[3] https://www.worldhistory.org/Pylos

[4] https://journals.akademicka.pl/saac/article/download/1109/949/1786
──────────────────────────────── Final Report ─────────────────────────────────
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                               Ring of Nestor                                │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

The Ring of Nestor is the name used in scholarship for a gold signet ring      
associated with the Griffin Warrior burial at Pylos, Messenia, Greece.[1][2][3]
The burial, found near the Palace of Nestor, contained the remains of a man    
aged about 30-35 and hundreds of grave goods, including four gold signet       
rings.[2][3] In the literature, the ring has been discussed for its iconography
and for questions of dating and authenticity.[1][2] The source set also        
contains three disclosed inconsistencies, x1, x2, and x3: one extract gives    
differing summaries of the ring's iconography, another gives inconsistent size 
notation for the Pylos Combat Agate, and a third gives different dates for     
comparison seals used in the broader discussion.                               


                           Discovery and provenance                            

In May 2015, a University of Cincinnati team discovered a large stone-built    
tomb of Late Helladic IIA date near Tholos Tomb IV at the Palace of Nestor     
during renewed excavations at Pylos.[2][3] The tomb is usually referred to as  
the Griffin Warrior grave, after the adult male buried there, and its grave    
goods included objects of gold, silver, bronze, ivory, and semiprecious        
stone.[2] Among them were four gold signet rings.[2][3] Pylos was a major      
Mycenaean center in Messenia and the seat of the Palace of Nestor on the hill  
of Ano Englianos.[3] The source set also records three disclosed               
inconsistencies, x1, x2, and x3: one extract gives differing summaries of the  
ring's iconography, another gives inconsistent size notation for the Pylos     
Combat Agate, and a third gives different dates for comparison seals; these    
points belong to the wider comparative discussion rather than the ring's       
provenance.                                                                    


                 Description, iconography, and interpretation                  

A 2013 article by Theodore Eliopoulos, titled "Some Observations on the        
Iconography of the Ring of Nestor," discusses the ring in the context of       
iconography and authenticity debates.[1] In the Griffin Warrior burial, the    
four gold signet rings are described as decorated with Minoan ritual scenes,   
and their imagery has been treated as relevant to Mycenaean interpretations of 
Minoan religious iconography.[2] Stocker and Davis describe the Pylos Combat   
Agate as a Cretan work of Late Minoan I found with other sealstones and four   
gold rings near the warrior's right arm, which helps distinguish it from the   
ring discussed here.[4] The comparative literature cited in this discussion is 
not uniform: CMS dates related seals such as CMS I no. 11 and CMS I no. 16 to  
LB I-II, while Vonhoff dates them to LM IIIA/B and LH IIIA/B; the source set   
also includes x1, x2, and x3 on iconographic interpretation, size notation, and
comparison-seal chronology.[4][5]                                              


